<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jinnō Shōtōki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1339">1339</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jinnō Shōtōki ('Chronicle of the Direct Descent of Gods and Sovereigns') is a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitabatake-chikafusa">Kitabatake Chikafusa</a> in 1339 and revised in 1343. Tracing the imperial line from the age of the gods, it argued that the Southern Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a>'s descendants held the rightful succession. It became a classic of Japanese political thought and later inspired imperial loyalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jinno-shotoki.jpg" alt="Jinnō Shōtōki" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kitabatake_Chikafusa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
